Operator Procedence:
Operator Precedence is the order in which python evalutes operator in an expression.
If an expression contains multiple operators, Python follows a predefined order to decide which operator is performed first.

Order of Operator Precedence(Highest to Lowest)
Parentheses(), Exponent(Power **), Unary Plus(+x), Unary Minus(-x), Multiplication(*), Division(/), Floor Division(//),
Modulus(%),Addition(+), Subtraction(-), Comparision Operators((==),(!=),>,<,>=,<=), Logical NOT(not), Logical AND(and)
Logical OR(or)

Easy Memory Trick(PEMDAS)
P - Parentheses()
E - Exponent(**)
M - Multiplication(*)
D - Division(/), Floor Division(//), Modulus(%)
A - Addition(+)
S - Subtraction(-)

Parentheses() are evaluated first.
Exponent(**) is evaluated before multiplication and division.
(*),(/),(//),(%) are evaluated before (+) and (-).
Comparision operators are evaluated after arthematic operators.
Logical Operators are evaluated last (not-->and-->or).
